## `git fetch`

Downloads latest changes from the remote repository **without merging** them into your current branch.

```bash id="1t5s9e"
git fetch
```

Example:

```bash id="5q2kpa"
git fetch origin
```

Use this when you want to:

* see remote changes first
* review differences before merging

You can compare changes after fetch:

```bash id="r4v8mb"
git log HEAD..origin/main --oneline
```

---

## `git pull`

Fetches changes **and automatically merges** them into your current branch.

```bash id="u6m3zs"
git pull
```

Example:

```bash id="k2n9fd"
git pull origin main
```

Equivalent to:

```bash id="v7a1xe"
git fetch
git merge
```

---

## Main difference

| Command     | Downloads Changes | Merges Automatically |
| ----------- | ----------------- | -------------------- |
| `git fetch` | Yes               | No                   |
| `git pull`  | Yes               | Yes                  |

---

## Recommended workflow

If working on important code:

```bash id="f0w7lc"
git fetch
git diff origin/main
```

Then merge manually if everything looks good.

